.Dd August 18, 2026
.Dt LOCALE.CONF 5
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm locale.conf
.Nd the locale a login shell starts in
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /etc/locale.conf
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The C library has no configuration file of its own.
.Xr setlocale 3
is told what to do by the environment alone - by
.Ev LANG ,
by the
.Ev LC_*
variables and by
.Ev LC_ALL -
so something has to put those variables there.
On this system that something is
.Pa /etc/profile.d/locale.sh ,
and
.Pa /etc/locale.conf
is the file it reads.
.Pp
Each line is
.Pp
.Dl NAME=value
.Pp
with no spaces around the
.Ql = .
The value may be quoted.
Lines that name anything other than the variables below are ignored, which is
what makes a comment a comment.
.Bl -tag -width LC_IDENTIFICATION
.It Ev LANG
The default for every category.
This is the one most systems set and nothing else.
.It Ev LC_CTYPE
Character classification and case conversion - which is where the character set
lives, and therefore whether the system believes in UTF-8 at all.
.It Ev LC_COLLATE
Sort order.
.Ql C
sorts by code point;
a real locale sorts the way a dictionary in that language does.
.It Ev LC_TIME , Ev LC_NUMERIC , Ev LC_MONETARY , Ev LC_PAPER , Ev LC_MEASUREMENT
Formats: dates, decimal separators, currency, paper size, metric or not.
.It Ev LC_MESSAGES
The language of messages, for the programs that have translations.
Few in this image do - it is built without gettext where that was a choice - so
this mostly decides
.Xr yes 1 Ns 's
idea of what "yes" looks like.
.It Ev LC_NAME , Ev LC_ADDRESS , Ev LC_TELEPHONE , Ev LC_IDENTIFICATION
The remaining categories, which few programs read.
.It Ev LANGUAGE
Not a category: a colon-separated list of languages for message translation,
consulted before
.Ev LC_MESSAGES .
.It Ev LC_ALL
Overrides every category at once.
It is meant for a script that needs a fixed locale and is a poor thing to set
here, because it cannot then be overridden one category at a time.
.El
.Sh WHAT OVERRIDES WHAT
A value already in the environment is left alone.
.Xr sshd 8
is configured with
.Ql AcceptEnv LANG LC_* ,
so a person logging in from a machine that speaks their language keeps their own
locale rather than being handed the server's, and a script that exports
.Ev LC_ALL
before running something keeps it.
This file is the default, not the decision.
.Pp
Within the C library the order is
.Ev LC_ALL ,
then the specific category, then
.Ev LANG ,
then the
.Ql C
locale.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/profile.d/locale.sh"
.It Pa /etc/locale.conf
This file.
.It Pa /etc/profile.d/locale.sh
What reads it, for every login shell.
.It Pa /etc/locale.gen
Which locales exist on this machine to be named here.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A German system:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
locale-gen de_DE.UTF-8
printf 'LANG=de_DE.UTF-8\en' > /etc/locale.conf
.Ed
.Pp
A Polish system that writes ISO dates and sorts Polish:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
LANG=pl_PL.UTF-8
LC_TIME=en_DK.UTF-8
.Ed
.Sh CAVEATS
Naming a locale that has not been compiled is the common mistake.
The C library says nothing at the point of failure: every program prints
.Ql setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale
on startup and carries on in the
.Ql C
locale, so text stops being UTF-8 and nothing explains why.
.Xr locale-gen 8
warns about it,
.Ql locale -a
lists what is really there, and
.Ql locale
with no arguments prints what the current shell resolved to.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr locale 1 ,
.Xr locale.gen 5 ,
.Xr locale-gen 8
.Pp
.Pa /root/sowa-howto/locale.txt
